    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d759">

                <pb facs="0814" n="800" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg800"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.20210/11–1344</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bowers</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 11086</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-11-13">November 13,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received November 25.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    Department’s Secret Airgram No. A–673, October 27, 12:15 P.M., 1944,<anchor
                        n="83" xml:id="d759-f1"/> in which the Embassy is requested to submit a
                    despatch setting forth the present status of the PQZ espionage trial and, in the
                    event that a final decision has not been reached by the Courts, to make an
                    appropriate comment.</p>
                <p>There is enclosed a copy of a self-explanatory memorandum dated November 8,
                        1944,<anchor n="83" xml:id="d759-f2"/> which was prepared for me by the
                    Legal Attaché, <pb facs="0815" n="801" xml:id="pg801"/> who had been and is in
                    close touch with Investigaciones and with Señor Luis Baquedano Lira, the Judge
                    in charge of the case.</p>
                <p>It will be noted that on November 6th Judge Baquedano sent his complete findings
                    in the case, including the major facts, his opinion, and the sentences to be
                    applied to the persons implicated, to the Attorney of the Court of Appeals of
                    Santiago. Judge Baquedano told us that the Attorney would require two weeks to
                    give his opinion concerning the sentences which should be applied. It is my
                    understanding that the Judge is not by Chilean law bound to accept the opinions
                    of the Attorney but is free to lay down the sentences which he himself
                    recommended, if he so chooses.</p>
                <p>The Embassy would prefer not to comment on the case until Baquedano has studied
                    the report of the Attorney of the Court of Appeals and has made his final
                    decision. If this decision is unduly delayed, further inquiries will be made of
                    the Judge.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Claude G. Bowers</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
